<BaseLayout title="Editorial Standards | Around The House" description="How the Colorado-based Quiet Tools Support Team researches, reviews, updates, sources, and corrects Around The House homeowner resources." canonicalPath="/editorial-standards/" page="legal">
  <LegalArticle kicker="Trust and sourcing" title="Editorial Standards" effectiveDate="September 9, 2026">
    <p>All Around The House guides are researched, written, or reviewed by the Quiet Tools Support Team in Colorado, United States. These standards describe how we keep that content useful, appropriately sourced, and transparent about its relationship to the app.</p>
    <h2>Purpose and scope</h2>
    <p>We publish practical guidance about home maintenance planning and recordkeeping. Our articles help readers identify what to check, what to record, which instructions control, and when professional help is appropriate. They are general educational resources, not individualized engineering, legal, insurance, medical, emergency, or trade advice.</p>
    <h2>Source standards</h2>
    <p>Safety-sensitive and equipment-specific claims should rely on primary sources whenever available. Preferred sources include government agencies, public-safety authorities, standards-based public guidance, and the manufacturer of the equipment being discussed. Relevant sources are linked visibly on the article.</p>
    <p>Manufacturer instructions for the reader's exact model, current local codes, product labels, recalls, and qualified professional advice take priority over general website guidance.</p>
    <h2>Review and updates</h2>
    <p>Guides display a visible last-updated date. The team reviews content when product behavior changes, a cited source materially changes, a correction is reported, or periodic review identifies outdated guidance. Updating punctuation or formatting alone does not justify presenting an article as newly reviewed.</p>
    <h2>Safety boundaries</h2>
    <p>We identify material hazards and professional handoff points when they are relevant. Readers should stop and seek qualified help for work involving conditions outside their skills or instructions, including gas, combustion, household wiring, refrigerant, structures, roofing, difficult ladder access, active leaks, or damaged safety equipment.</p>
    <h2>Commercial transparency</h2>
    <p>Around The House resources are published by the team that builds and supports the app. Articles may link to product pages or describe app workflows. We do not portray those mentions as independent recommendations, and product claims should be supported by the current app experience.</p>
    <h2>Corrections</h2>
    <p>Send a suspected error, outdated source, or clarification request to <a href="mailto:support@quiettoolsapps.com">support@quiettoolsapps.com</a>. Include the page URL and the specific statement in question. The Quiet Tools Support Team will review the report and update the page when a correction is warranted.</p>
  </LegalArticle>
</BaseLayout>
